    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, can you help me write a brief for a stakeholder? Need to explain the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling in AWS. I want to keep it simple so they get why we need Auto Scaling groups for traffic spikes, but keep it professional. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, I'd be happy to help with that. Vertical scaling refers to increasing the resources (CPU, memory, etc.) of an existing server instance in AWS. Horizontal scaling involves adding more instances to your environment to handle increased load, and AWS Auto Scaling groups facilitate this by automatically adjusting the number of instances across instances, availability zones, and regions.\n\nWhen coordinated effectively, these adjustments can accommodate traffic spikes without affecting service stability. Auto Scaling's ability to react to changing demand is crucial for both cost management and maintaining consistent application performance, offering businesses an optimal balance between resource availability and cost-efficiency.",
