    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you maybe expand a bit more on the \"risks\" of vertical scaling? Like, why can't we just keep making the single server bigger and bigger whenever we need to? I want to make sure they understand the limitations.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely. Vertical scaling, also known as \"scaling up,\" includes boosting the capacity of an existing server instance — such as increasing CPU, memory, or storage. While it's straightforward, it encounters intrinsic limitations: there's only so much you can grow a single server before you face physical constraints and diminishing returns. Bigger servers also increase dependency on a single point of failure.\n\nAnother critical issue with vertical scaling is that downtime during upgrades is inevitable, which could isolate the node from the cluster. Additionally, there's a cost implication, as larger instances tend to be pricier in the long term. Because of these reasons, it's prudent to complement vertical scaling in AWS with horizontal scaling using Auto Scaling groups, enhancing overall application resilience and infrastructure scalability.",
      "source": "target",
